the target language is: prolog.

% A recursion through a value not yet known (docs/user/reference/language.md
% §2.4). A loan's repayment obligation starts again after every payment made
% within 40 days of the day the current obligation started. Asked "on which
% days does an obligation start?", the rule must ask the same question about
% an earlier, unknown day. Without the marker, the check against endless
% loops refuses that inner question as soon as it would need the rule again,
% so only the payments that follow the first day are found: 2025-01-01 and
% 2025-02-05, but not 2025-03-12 or 2025-04-15, which follow later days.
% With the template marked `; memorable`, the inner question is answered from
% the answers found so far, round after round, until no new one appears:
% every day is found. The payment of 2025-06-30 comes more than 40 days after
% the last obligation started, and starts nothing.

the templates are:
    the borrower pays on *a date*; undefined.
    an obligation starts on *a date*; memorable.
    a payment on *a date* is within 40 days of *a date*.

the knowledge base instalments includes:

an obligation starts on 2025-01-01.

an obligation starts on a date if
    an obligation starts on an earlier date
    and the borrower pays on the date
    and a payment on the date is within 40 days of the earlier date.

a payment on a date is within 40 days of an earlier date if
    the date is after the earlier date
    and a limit is 40 days after the earlier date
    and the date is before or equal to the limit.

query starts is:
    an obligation starts on which date.

scenario monthly is:
    the borrower pays on 2025-02-05.
    the borrower pays on 2025-03-12.
    the borrower pays on 2025-04-15.
    the borrower pays on 2025-06-30.
    starts expects answers ["an obligation starts on 2025-01-01", "an obligation starts on 2025-02-05", "an obligation starts on 2025-03-12", "an obligation starts on 2025-04-15"].
